[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: A command-line tool that is the only writer of sdoc files

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[SLICE]
UID: SLICE-SDOC-CLI
TITLE: A command-line tool that is the only writer of sdoc files
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Build the command-line tool that produces and edits sdoc documents. Its option
surface is DERIVED from the generated grammar: one flag per field a node type
declares, one per relation role it may make, and choices constrained to that
field's word list. Nothing about what a node may contain is typed by a person.

Writing verbs are new, set, relate, unrelate, move and delete. Reading verbs are
show, list and check. Every writing verb loads the whole graph, applies the
change in memory, validates the graph as it would be AFTER the write, formats
through the same code path, and only then replaces the file. A command that
cannot validate does not write. Every writing verb takes --dry-run and prints
the diff instead.

UIDs are passed in by the author and never minted -- they are hand-chosen and
semantic by the operator's 2026-08-27 ruling, and the tool refuses a name whose
prefix does not match the node type. Required fields are filled from arguments,
so a TBD never lands.

NO SEMANTIC RULES APPLY AT THIS MILESTONE, and that is the scope boundary. The
tool enforces exactly what the GRAMMAR says -- the node types, which fields are
required, each choice field's word list, and which relation roles a type may
declare. It enforces nothing about how this instance USES them. Who may sign,
whether DEPTH may regress, when deleting is legitimate, and where direct edits
are denied are all instance semantics, and milestone five is where they are
written.

delete exists in the tool and is deliberately NOT taught by the sdoc skill. The
verb is available to someone who wants it; nothing points an agent at it.

Moved out of this milestone rather than dropped: denying direct edits of sdoc
and grammar files in the agent harness before the edit tool runs. That is a
checkpoint, and milestone four owns checkpoints.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
In the operator's words, the root failure is a model producing incorrect formats
with nothing validating the write; the tool takes the model's hands off the
files. Measured: creating a node in-process costs 0.35 s, validating the whole
graph 0.3 s, and strictdoc's node model writes any field in grammar order --
nothing here waits on the behaviour model.

Deriving the option surface from the grammar rather than hand-writing it is what
makes the milestone small. The five node types are genuinely asymmetric -- a
DECISION carries no DEPTH and no File relation, a SPIKE is the only type
carrying both a DEPTH and a STATUS with a different word list under the same
name, an INVARIANT cannot point at a file -- and that asymmetry is exactly what
gets hand-written wrong.
<<<
PARENT_FP: DEC-EXECUTABLE-RULES-ROADMAP:0000000
NOTES: >>>
SCOPE SETTLED BY THE OPERATOR 2026-08-27, and it collapsed. Their framing: the
grammar defines the CLI options for everything but delete, the tool runs format
and validation, and that is the milestone -- "simple cli to produce docs from
options. no semantic rules apply yet."

Three questions raised against the draft verb list were ruled INSTANCE SEMANTICS
and are not this milestone's: whether deleting is legitimate, whether raising
DEPTH is an ordinary edit or a lifecycle move, and whether to ship the harness
denial given it is absent in eleven of twenty-three worktrees. All three sit
with milestone five.

Build on strictdoc's own machinery rather than re-implementing writes: manage
new for scaffolding, set_field_value for fields, the writer for formatting. Two
measured constraints on that. set_field_value performs NO value validation --
DEPTH='totally-not-a-depth' and a None on a required STATEMENT both serialise
cleanly and reach disk -- so the validation has to be the tool's, not the
model's. And single-line versus block form is chosen from the grammar rather
than from what was parsed, so a PARENT_FP written through the model is always a
block while the corpus carries inline single-entry values; that is hash-neutral
because PARENT_FP is in EXCLUDED_FIELDS, but it is a visible diff.

Validating the whole graph per command costs about a second on this corpus and
about two at five thousand nodes when strictdoc's cache is warm, which needs the
working copy at a stable path; a fresh temporary copy pays the cold cost.

Prefix-to-type agreement is unenforced today: a MECHANISM named SLICE-... exports
exit 0 and lands in the JSON unremarked. The tool is where that closes.
<<<
